{
  "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
  "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 11:35 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Roberto A. Lange",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Stay and abeyance is the proper way to administer the total exhaustion rule under the unique facts of this case. Several Justices of this Court already have endorsed stay and abeyance, and seven of the eight circuit courts of appeals that have considered the issue have permitted stay and abeyance under similar circumstances."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, why shouldn't -- why shouldn't the petitioner for habeas have to first go to the State court and exhaust the State court claims? Here that wasn't done."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Roberto A. Lange",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I disagree that that is a free ride, and I do not see that as a frustration of the 1-year statute of limitations when a petitioner has met the 1-year statute of limitations."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0001-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "First, in the 3 and a half years since Duncan v. Walker was decided, there have been cases in circuit courts of appeals that have approved of the stay and abeyance procedure. None of those cases evidence that it is being misused or is causing undue delay."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "However, stay and abeyance erodes each side of this triangular statutory design. Stay and abeyance allows tolling in Federal court when Duncan said there should be none. It extends the statute of limitations beyond 1 year, and it ignores the total exhaustion requirement of 2254(b)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0001-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 27,
        "start": 27
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One of the answers to the observation that the Federal court is closed to a third of the claims is that that's not true with respect to the exhausted claims."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Why should that be?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "General Long",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, I think the predicate to getting stay and abeyance, Your Honor, is that there has to be at least the presence of one exhausted claim and one unexhausted -"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice O'Connor",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0002-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE O'CONNOR: General Long, if you were designing a system to set standards to guide the Federal judge in deciding how to treat these, what standards would you set?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Lange",
      "case_id": "2004-03-9046",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "de18b96983ea01ba3ab98573a30647660d9572248433428bdf85a14a25359d9d",
      "evidence_id": "ev-de18b96983ea-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I believe that a district court, in evaluating a request for stay and abeyance, should look to make sure that the petition meets the standards of section 2254(b)(2) which is the provision that allows a district court to reach the merits of unexhausted claims to deny them."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
